{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21734","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21734","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21734","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Guan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changling","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Changling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sen","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasin Abbasi","familyName":"Yadkori","name":"Yadkori, Yasin Abbasi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hierarchical Reasoning Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T19:39:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:06:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-22T06:45:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-23T00:22:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-04T08:45:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08-05T01:15:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-30T03:25:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-01T00:39:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning, the process of devising and executing complex goal-oriented action sequences, remains a critical challenge in AI. Current large language models (LLMs) primarily employ Chain-of-Thought (CoT) techniques, which suffer from brittle task decomposition, extensive data requirements, and high latency. Inspired by the hierarchical and multi-timescale processing in the human brain, we propose the Hierarchical Reasoning Model (HRM), a novel recurrent architecture that attains significant computational depth while maintaining both training stability and efficiency. HRM executes sequential reasoning tasks in a single forward pass without explicit supervision of the intermediate process, through two interdependent recurrent modules: a high-level module responsible for slow, abstract planning, and a low-level module handling rapid, detailed computations. With only 27 million parameters, HRM achieves exceptional performance on complex reasoning tasks using only 1000 training samples. The model operates without pre-training or CoT data, yet achieves nearly perfect performance on challenging tasks including complex Sudoku puzzles and optimal path finding in large mazes. Furthermore, HRM outperforms much larger models with significantly longer context windows on the Abstraction and Reasoning Corpus (ARC), a key benchmark for measuring artificial general intelligence capabilities. These results underscore HRM's potential as a transformative advancement toward universal computation and general-purpose reasoning systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21734","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:25:39Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:25:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-01T03:45:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21833","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21833","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21833","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunjal","familyName":"Panchal","name":"Panchal, Kunjal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunav","familyName":"Choudhary","name":"Choudhary, Sunav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuriy","familyName":"Brun","name":"Brun, Yuriy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Memory Savings at What Cost? A Study of Alternatives to Backpropagation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T00:47:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:11:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-10T19:11:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-14T00:05:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Forward-mode automatic differentiation (FmAD) and zero-order (ZO) optimization are increasingly proposed as memory-efficient, backpropagation-free alternatives for large language model (LLM) fine-tuning, yet their benefits are typically evaluated only against standard backpropagation (BP), omitting memory-efficient variants such as activation checkpointing. We present a unified theoretical and empirical comparison of BP, checkpointed BP, FmAD, and ZO for LLM and vision-language model training, showing that while FmAD and ZO reduce activation memory, they trade memory for higher computational cost and longer wall-clock time to convergence, resulting in lower accuracy and slower training, especially under constrained perturbation budgets. Across models, BP with checkpointing outperforms FmAD and ZO variants, including variance-reduced methods, achieving up to 31.1% higher accuracy, 34.8% faster convergence, and 3.8x fewer computations at comparable memory usage, while also revealing instability-related failure modes in FmAD and ZO. Overall, our results correct a one-sided benchmarking narrative by showing that memory-efficient methods entail fundamentally different trade-offs, and that ignoring these distinctions has led to misleading conclusions about LLM optimization in prior work. Our source code is available at {https://github.com/Astuary/Gradient_Estimation_Methods}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21833","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:28:03Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:28:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-14T03:56:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.02935","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.02935","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.02935","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fardin","familyName":"Saad","name":"Saad, Fardin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pradeep K.","familyName":"Murukannaiah","name":"Murukannaiah, Pradeep K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Munindar P.","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Munindar P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Theory of Mind in Action: The Instruction Inference Task in Dynamic Human-Agent Collaboration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T20:44:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-08T00:01:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-16T03:54:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T00:24:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T02:59:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:21:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Successful human-agent teaming relies on an agent being able to understand instructions given by a (human) principal. In many cases, an instruction may be incomplete or ambiguous. In such cases, the agent must infer the unspoken intentions from their shared context, that is, it must exercise the principal's Theory of Mind (ToM) and infer the mental states of its principal. We consider the prospects of effective human-agent collaboration using large language models (LLMs). To assess ToM in a dynamic, goal-oriented, and collaborative environment, we introduce a novel task, Instruction Inference, in which an agent assists a principal in reaching a goal by interpreting incomplete or ambiguous instructions. We present Tomcat, an LLM-based agent, designed to exhibit ToM reasoning in interpreting and responding to the principal's instructions. We implemented two variants of Tomcat. One, dubbed Fs-CoT (Fs for few-shot, CoT for chain-of-thought), is based on a small number of examples demonstrating the requisite structured reasoning. One, dubbed CP (commonsense prompt), relies on commonsense knowledge and information about the problem. We realized both variants of Tomcat on three leading LLMs, namely, GPT-4o, DeepSeek-R1, and Gemma-3-27B. To evaluate the effectiveness of Tomcat, we conducted a study with 52 human participants in which we provided participants with the same information as the CP variant. We computed intent accuracy, action optimality, and planning optimality to measure the ToM capabilities of Tomcat and our study participants. We found that Tomcat with Fs-CoT, particularly with GPT-4o and DeepSeek-R1, achieves performance comparable to the human participants, underscoring its ToM potential for human-agent collaboration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"66 pages with appendix, 10 figures (Appendix: 26 Figures), 11 tables. Code available at: https://github.com/fardinsaad/Tomcat-LLM"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.02935","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-08T03:09:02Z","registered":"2025-07-08T03:09:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:07:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21656","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21656","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21656","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanzhe","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuanzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyuan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiao","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yixiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenming","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Wenming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James Matthew","familyName":"Rehg","name":"Rehg, James Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ismini","familyName":"Lourentzou","name":"Lourentzou, Ismini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-Grained Preference Optimization Improves Spatial Reasoning in VLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T18:00:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:02:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-26T22:18:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:12:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-05T03:33:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-06T01:57:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current Vision-Language Models (VLMs) struggle with fine-grained spatial reasoning, particularly when multi-step logic and precise spatial alignment are required. In this work, we introduce SpatialReasoner-R1, a vision-language reasoning model designed to address these limitations. To construct high-quality supervision for spatial reasoning, we design a Multi-Model Monte Carlo Tree Search (M3CTS) method that generates diverse, logically consistent Long Chain-of-Thought (LongCOT) reasoning trajectories. In addition, we propose a fine-grained Direct Preference Optimization (fDPO) method that introduces segment-specific preference granularity for descriptive grounding and logical reasoning, guided by a spatial reward mechanism that evaluates candidate responses based on visual consistency, spatial grounding, and logical coherence. Experimental results demonstrate that fDPO achieves relative performance gains of 4.1% and 9.0% over standard DPO on spatial qualitative and quantitative tasks, respectively. SpatialReasoner-R1, trained with fDPO, sets a new SoTA on SpatialRGPT-Bench, outperforming the strongest baseline by 9.4% in average accuracy, while maintaining competitive performance on general vision-language tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21656","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:23:50Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:23:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-06T04:06:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.00057","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.00057","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.00057","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Valentin","name":"Valentin, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ardi","familyName":"Madadi","name":"Madadi, Ardi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaetano","familyName":"Sapia","name":"Sapia, Gaetano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcel","familyName":"Böhme","name":"Böhme, Marcel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Incoherence as Oracle-less Measure of Error in LLM-Based Code Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T22:00:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-02T00:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-13T19:27:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T01:39:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating code from a natural language programming task is one of the most successful applications of Large Language Models (LLMs). Yet, the generated program may be buggy. Without an oracle, such as an existing, correct implementation or a formal specification, can we somehow estimate how likely the generated program is correct?\n In this paper, we propose a measure of incorrectness, called *incoherence*, that can be estimated efficiently in the absence of an oracle and allows us to establish a lower bound on the error, i.e., the probability that the LLM-generated program for that specification is incorrect. In our experiments, our incoherence-based methodology can automatically identify about two-thirds of incorrect programs without reports of false positives for the average task. In fact, *an oracle-based evaluation of LLMs can be reliably replaced by an incoherence-based evaluation*. In particular, we find a very strong agreement between the ranking of LLMs by the number of programs deemed correct via an oracle (pass@1) and the ranking of LLMs by the number of programs deemed correct via incoherence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at AAAI'26 (extended version). 8 pages + refs and appendix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.00057","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-02T01:32:02Z","registered":"2025-07-02T01:32:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:46:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21714","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21714","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21714","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Denis","familyName":"Gudovskiy","name":"Gudovskiy, Denis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenzhao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Wenzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomoyuki","familyName":"Okuno","name":"Okuno, Tomoyuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yohei","familyName":"Nakata","name":"Nakata, Yohei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kurt","familyName":"Keutzer","name":"Keutzer, Kurt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ODE$_t$(ODE$_l$): Shortcutting the Time and the Length in Diffusion and Flow Models for Faster Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T18:59:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:05:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-02T20:53:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-04T00:07:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T00:50:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:12:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Continuous normalizing flows (CNFs) and diffusion models (DMs) generate high-quality data from a noise distribution. However, their sampling process demands multiple iterations to solve an ordinary differential equation (ODE) with high computational complexity. State-of-the-art methods focus on reducing the number of discrete time steps during sampling to improve efficiency. In this work, we explore a complementary direction in which the quality-complexity tradeoff can also be controlled in terms of the neural network length. We achieve this by rewiring the blocks in the transformer-based architecture to solve an inner discretized ODE w.r.t. its depth. Then, we apply a length consistency term during flow matching training, and as a result, the sampling can be performed with an arbitrary number of time steps and transformer blocks. Unlike others, our ODE$_t$(ODE$_l$) approach is solver-agnostic in time dimension and reduces both latency and, importantly, memory usage. CelebA-HQ and ImageNet generation experiments show a latency reduction of up to $2\\times$ in the most efficient sampling mode, and FID improvement of up to $2.8$ points for high-quality sampling when applied to prior methods. We open-source our code and checkpoints at github.com/gudovskiy/odelt."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to WACV 2026. Preprint. Github page: github.com/gudovskiy/odelt"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21714","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:25:11Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:25:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-19T03:14:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangyu","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Liangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Rosenthal","name":"Rosenthal, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joachim","familyName":"Sicking","name":"Sicking, Joachim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Hüger","name":"Hüger, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thorsten","familyName":"Bagdonat","name":"Bagdonat, Thorsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanno","familyName":"Gottschalk","name":"Gottschalk, Hanno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leo","familyName":"Schwinn","name":"Schwinn, Leo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations for Efficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T18:51:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:05:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T14:46:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:53:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) offer strong perception and reasoning capabilities for image-text input, Visual Question Answering (VQA) focusing on small image details still remains a challenge. Although visual cropping techniques seem promising, recent approaches have several limitations: the need for task-specific fine-tuning, low efficiency due to uninformed exhaustive search, or incompatibility with efficient attention implementations. We address these shortcomings by proposing a training-free visual cropping method, dubbed FOCUS, that leverages MLLM-internal representations to guide the search for the most relevant image region. This is accomplished in four steps: first, we identify the target object(s) in the VQA prompt; second, we compute an object relevance map using the key-value (KV) cache; third, we propose and rank relevant image regions based on the map; and finally, we perform the fine-grained VQA task using the top-ranked region. As a result of this informed search strategy, FOCUS achieves strong performance across four fine-grained VQA datasets and three types of MLLMs. It outperforms three popular visual cropping methods in both accuracy and efficiency, and matches the best-performing baseline, ZoomEye, while requiring 3 - 6.5 x less compute."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025 - main track. Project page: https://focus-mllm-vqa.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:25:05Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:25:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:15:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.02937","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.02937","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.02937","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sotirios Panagiotis","familyName":"Chytas","name":"Chytas, Sotirios Panagiotis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rudrasis","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Rudrasis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikas","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Vikas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FoGE: Fock Space inspired encoding for graph prompting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T23:48:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-08T00:01:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T19:36:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:07:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent results show that modern Large Language Models (LLM) are indeed capable of understanding and answering questions about structured data such as graphs. This new paradigm can lead to solutions that require less supervision while, at the same time, providing a model that can generalize and answer questions beyond the training labels. Existing proposals often use some description of the graph to create an ``augmented'' prompt fed to the LLM. For a chosen class of graphs, if a well-tailored graph encoder is deployed to play together with a pre-trained LLM, the model can answer graph-related questions well. Existing solutions to graph-based prompts range from graph serialization to graph transformers. In this work, we show that the use of a parameter-free graph encoder based on Fock space representations, a concept borrowed from mathematical physics, is remarkably versatile in this problem setting. The simple construction, inherited directly from the theory with a few small adjustments, can provide rich and informative graph encodings, for a wide range of different graphs. We investigate the use of this idea for prefix-tuned prompts leveraging the capabilities of a pre-trained, frozen LLM. The modifications lead to a model that can answer graph-related questions -- from simple graphs to proteins to hypergraphs -- effectively and with minimal, if any, adjustments to the architecture. Our work significantly simplifies existing solutions and generalizes well to multiple different graph-based structures effortlessly."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.02937","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-08T03:09:05Z","registered":"2025-07-08T03:09:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:21:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21741","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21741","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21741","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Sterling","name":"Sterling, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chad","familyName":"Gueli","name":"Gueli, Chad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mónica F.","familyName":"Bugallo","name":"Bugallo, Mónica F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Critically-Damped Higher-Order Langevin Dynamics for Generative Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T19:50:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:06:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-19T23:28:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T01:02:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Denoising diffusion probabilistic models (DDPMs) represent an entirely new class of generative AI methods that have yet to be fully explored. They use Langevin dynamics, represented as stochastic differential equations, to describe a process that transforms data into noise, the forward process, and a process that transforms noise into generated data, the reverse process. Many of these methods utilize auxiliary variables that formulate the data as a ``position\" variable, and the auxiliary variables are referred to as ``velocity\", ``acceleration\", etc. In this sense, it is possible to ``critically damp\" the dynamics. Critical damping has been successfully introduced in Critically-Damped Langevin Dynamics (CLD) and Critically-Damped Third-Order Langevin Dynamics (TOLD++), but has not yet been applied to dynamics of arbitrary order. The proposed methodology generalizes Higher-Order Langevin Dynamics (HOLD), a recent state-of-the-art diffusion method, by introducing the concept of critical damping from systems analysis. Similarly to TOLD++, this work proposes an optimal set of hyperparameters in the $n$-dimensional case, where HOLD leaves these to be user defined. Additionally, this work provides closed-form solutions for the mean and covariance of the forward process that greatly simplify its implementation. Experiments are performed on the CIFAR-10 and CelebA-HQ $256 \\times 256$ datasets, and validated against the FID metric."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21741","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:25:48Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:25:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:37:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21853","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21853","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21853","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dewei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenjia","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Chenjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyuan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Skill-Nav: Enhanced Navigation with Versatile Quadrupedal Locomotion via Waypoint Interface"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T02:08:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:13:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T08:59:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:08:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-07T09:44:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-09T00:40:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quadrupedal robots have demonstrated exceptional locomotion capabilities through Reinforcement Learning (RL), including extreme parkour maneuvers. However, integrating locomotion skills with navigation in quadrupedal robots has not been fully investigated, which holds promise for enhancing long-distance movement capabilities. In this paper, we propose Skill-Nav, a method that incorporates quadrupedal locomotion skills into a hierarchical navigation framework using waypoints as an interface. Specifically, we train a waypoint-guided locomotion policy using deep RL, enabling the robot to autonomously adjust its locomotion skills to reach targeted positions while avoiding obstacles. Compared with direct velocity commands, waypoints offer a simpler yet more flexible interface for high-level planning and low-level control. Utilizing waypoints as the interface allows for the application of various general planning tools, such as large language models (LLMs) and path planning algorithms, to guide our locomotion policy in traversing terrains with diverse obstacles. Extensive experiments conducted in both simulated and real-world scenarios demonstrate that Skill-Nav can effectively traverse complex terrains and complete challenging navigation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21853","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:28:32Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:28:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-09T03:15:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22521","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22521","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22521","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixiang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Kaixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lincan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lincan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaize","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Kaize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neil Zhenqiang","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Neil Zhenqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushun","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yushun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey on Model Extraction Attacks and Defenses for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T22:02:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-09T00:21:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Model extraction attacks pose significant security threats to deployed language models, potentially compromising intellectual property and user privacy. This survey provides a comprehensive taxonomy of LLM-specific extraction attacks and defenses, categorizing attacks into functionality extraction, training data extraction, and prompt-targeted attacks. We analyze various attack methodologies including API-based knowledge distillation, direct querying, parameter recovery, and prompt stealing techniques that exploit transformer architectures. We then examine defense mechanisms organized into model protection, data privacy protection, and prompt-targeted strategies, evaluating their effectiveness across different deployment scenarios. We propose specialized metrics for evaluating both attack effectiveness and defense performance, addressing the specific challenges of generative language models. Through our analysis, we identify critical limitations in current approaches and propose promising research directions, including integrated attack methodologies and adaptive defense mechanisms that balance security with model utility. This work serves NLP researchers, ML engineers, and security professionals seeking to protect language models in production environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22521","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:24:06Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:24:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-09T02:07:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxing","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jiaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xihan","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Xihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qibin","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Qibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLaVA-Scissor: Token Compression with Semantic Connected Components for Video LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T02:29:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:14:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we present LLaVA-Scissor, a training-free token compression strategy designed for video multimodal large language models. Previous methods mostly attempt to compress tokens based on attention scores, but fail to effectively capture all semantic regions and often lead to token redundancy. Differently, we propose to leverage the Semantic Connected Components (SCC) approach that assigns tokens to distinct semantic regions within the token set, ensuring comprehensive semantic coverage. The outcome is a two-step spatio-temporal token compression strategy that utilizes SCC in both spatial and temporal domains. This strategy can effectively compress tokens by representing the entire video with a set of non-overlapping semantic tokens. We conduct extensive evaluations of the token compression capabilities of LLaVA-Scissor across diverse video understanding benchmarks, including video question answering, long video understanding, and comprehensive multi-choices benchmarks. Experimental results show that the proposed LLaVA-Scissor outperforms other token compression methods, achieving superior performance in various video understanding benchmarks, particularly at low token retention ratios. Project page: https://github.com/HumanMLLM/LLaVA-Scissor."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 4 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:28:44Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:28:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:28:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21861","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21861","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21861","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taiga","familyName":"Someya","name":"Someya, Taiga","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryo","familyName":"Yoshida","name":"Yoshida, Ryo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hitomi","familyName":"Yanaka","name":"Yanaka, Hitomi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yohei","familyName":"Oseki","name":"Oseki, Yohei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Derivational Probing: Unveiling the Layer-wise Derivation of Syntactic Structures in Neural Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T02:29:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:13:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work has demonstrated that neural language models encode syntactic structures in their internal representations, yet the derivations by which these structures are constructed across layers remain poorly understood. In this paper, we propose Derivational Probing to investigate how micro-syntactic structures (e.g., subject noun phrases) and macro-syntactic structures (e.g., the relationship between the root verbs and their direct dependents) are constructed as word embeddings propagate upward across layers. Our experiments on BERT reveal a clear bottom-up derivation: micro-syntactic structures emerge in lower layers and are gradually integrated into a coherent macro-syntactic structure in higher layers. Furthermore, a targeted evaluation on subject-verb number agreement shows that the timing of constructing macro-syntactic structures is critical for downstream performance, suggesting an optimal timing for integrating global syntactic information."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21861","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:28:43Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:28:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:28:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21849","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21849","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21849","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debarun","familyName":"Bhattacharjya","name":"Bhattacharjya, Debarun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Balaji","familyName":"Ganesan","name":"Ganesan, Balaji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radu","familyName":"Marinescu","name":"Marinescu, Radu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katsiaryna","familyName":"Mirylenka","name":"Mirylenka, Katsiaryna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nhan H","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Nhan H","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Glass","name":"Glass, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junkyu","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Junkyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Consistency Hypothesis in Uncertainty Quantification for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T01:53:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:12:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Estimating the confidence of large language model (LLM) outputs is essential for real-world applications requiring high user trust. Black-box uncertainty quantification (UQ) methods, relying solely on model API access, have gained popularity due to their practical benefits. In this paper, we examine the implicit assumption behind several UQ methods, which use generation consistency as a proxy for confidence, an idea we formalize as the consistency hypothesis. We introduce three mathematical statements with corresponding statistical tests to capture variations of this hypothesis and metrics to evaluate LLM output conformity across tasks. Our empirical investigation, spanning 8 benchmark datasets and 3 tasks (question answering, text summarization, and text-to-SQL), highlights the prevalence of the hypothesis under different settings. Among the statements, we highlight the `Sim-Any' hypothesis as the most actionable, and demonstrate how it can be leveraged by proposing data-free black-box UQ methods that aggregate similarities between generations for confidence estimation. These approaches can outperform the closest baselines, showcasing the practical value of the empirically observed consistency hypothesis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by The Conference on Uncertainty in Artificial Intelligence (UAI) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21849","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:28:26Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:28:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:28:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21783","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21783","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21783","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandru","familyName":"Dumitru","name":"Dumitru, Alexandru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V","familyName":"Venktesh","name":"Venktesh, V","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Jatowt","name":"Jatowt, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avishek","familyName":"Anand","name":"Anand, Avishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating List Construction and Temporal Understanding capabilities of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T21:40:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:09:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated immense advances in a wide range of natural language tasks. However, these models are susceptible to hallucinations and errors on particularly temporal understanding tasks involving multiple entities in answers. In such tasks, they fail to associate entities with accurate time intervals, generate a complete list of entities in answers or reason about events associated with specific temporal bounds. Existing works do not extensively evaluate the abilities of the model to perform implicit and explicit temporal understanding in a list answer construction setup. To bridge this gap, we propose the Time referenced List based Question Answering or TLQA benchmark that requires structured answers in list format aligned with corresponding time periods. Our TLQA benchmark, requires both list construction and temporal understanding simultaneously, which to the best of our knowledge has not been explored in prior benchmarks. We investigate the temporal understanding and list construction capabilities of state-of-the-art generative models on TLQA in closed-book and open-domain settings. Our findings reveal significant shortcomings in current models, particularly their inability to provide complete answers and temporally align facts in a closed-book setup and the need to improve retrieval in open-domain setup, providing clear future directions for research on TLQA. The benchmark and code at https://github.com/elixir-research-group/TLQA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICTIR 2025 co-located with SIGIR 2025, 11 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21783","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:26:52Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:26:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:26:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21745","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21745","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21745","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eivind Morris","familyName":"Bakke","name":"Bakke, Eivind Morris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nora Winger","familyName":"Heggelund","name":"Heggelund, Nora Winger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"(Fact) Check Your Bias"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T20:03:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:06:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automatic fact verification systems increasingly rely on large language models (LLMs). We investigate how parametric knowledge biases in these models affect fact-checking outcomes of the HerO system (baseline for FEVER-25). We examine how the system is affected by: (1) potential bias in Llama 3.1's parametric knowledge and (2) intentionally injected bias. When prompted directly to perform fact-verification, Llama 3.1 labels nearly half the claims as \"Not Enough Evidence\". Using only its parametric knowledge it is able to reach a verdict on the remaining half of the claims. In the second experiment, we prompt the model to generate supporting, refuting, or neutral fact-checking documents. These prompts significantly influence retrieval outcomes, with approximately 50\\% of retrieved evidence being unique to each perspective. Notably, the model sometimes refuses to generate supporting documents for claims it believes to be false, creating an inherent negative bias. Despite differences in retrieved evidence, final verdict predictions show stability across prompting strategies. The code is available at: https://github.com/eibakke/FEVER-8-Shared-Task"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21745","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:25:54Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:25:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:25:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21712","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21712","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21712","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tzu-Quan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Tzu-Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsi-Chun","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hsi-Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung-yi","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hung-yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Identifying Speaker Information in Feed-Forward Layers of Self-Supervised Speech Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T18:54:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:05:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, the impact of self-supervised speech Transformers has extended to speaker-related applications. However, little research has explored how these models encode speaker information. In this work, we address this gap by identifying neurons in the feed-forward layers that are correlated with speaker information. Specifically, we analyze neurons associated with k-means clusters of self-supervised features and i-vectors. Our analysis reveals that these clusters correspond to broad phonetic and gender classes, making them suitable for identifying neurons that represent speakers. By protecting these neurons during pruning, we can significantly preserve performance on speaker-related task, demonstrating their crucial role in encoding speaker information."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21712","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:25:08Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:25:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:25:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21682","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21682","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21682","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zefeng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zefeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haizhou","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do We Really Need GNNs with Explicit Structural Modeling? MLPs Suffice for Language Model Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T18:10:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:03:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Explicit structural information has been proven to be encoded by Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), serving as auxiliary knowledge to enhance model capabilities and improve performance in downstream NLP tasks. However, recent studies indicate that GNNs fail to fully utilize structural information, whereas Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs), despite lacking the message-passing mechanisms inherent to GNNs, exhibit a surprising ability in structure-aware tasks. Motivated by these findings, this paper introduces a comprehensive probing framework from an information-theoretic perspective. The framework is designed to systematically assess the role of explicit structural modeling in enhancing language model (LM) representations and to investigate the potential of MLPs as efficient and scalable alternatives to GNNs. We extend traditional probing classifiers by incorporating a control module that allows for selective use of either the full GNN model or its decoupled components, specifically, the message-passing and feature-transformation operations.This modular approach isolates and assesses the individual contributions of these operations, avoiding confounding effects from the complete GNN architecture. Using the Edge Probing Suite, a diagnostic tool for evaluating the linguistic knowledge encoded in LMs, we find that MLPs, when used as feature-transformation modules, consistently improve the linguistic knowledge captured in LM representations across different architectures. They effectively encode both syntactic and semantic patterns. Similarly, GNNs that incorporate feature-transformation operations show beneficial effects. In contrast, models that rely solely on message-passing operations tend to underperform, often leading to negative impacts on probing task performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Graph Neural Networks, Multi-Layer Perceptrons, Explicit Structural Modeling, Probing Classifier"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21682","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:24:27Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:24:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:24:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hakan","familyName":"Çapuk","name":"Çapuk, Hakan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Bond","name":"Bond, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammed Burak","familyName":"Kızıl","name":"Kızıl, Muhammed Burak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emir","familyName":"Göçen","name":"Göçen, Emir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erkut","familyName":"Erdem","name":"Erdem, Erkut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aykut","familyName":"Erdem","name":"Erdem, Aykut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TanDiT: Tangent-Plane Diffusion Transformer for High-Quality 360° Panorama Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T18:09:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:03:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in image generation have led to remarkable improvements in synthesizing perspective images. However, these models still struggle with panoramic image generation due to unique challenges, including varying levels of geometric distortion and the requirement for seamless loop-consistency. To address these issues while leveraging the strengths of the existing models, we introduce TanDiT, a method that synthesizes panoramic scenes by generating grids of tangent-plane images covering the entire 360$^\\circ$ view. Unlike previous methods relying on multiple diffusion branches, TanDiT utilizes a unified diffusion model trained to produce these tangent-plane images simultaneously within a single denoising iteration. Furthermore, we propose a model-agnostic post-processing step specifically designed to enhance global coherence across the generated panoramas. To accurately assess panoramic image quality, we also present two specialized metrics, TangentIS and TangentFID, and provide a comprehensive benchmark comprising captioned panoramic datasets and standardized evaluation scripts. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method generalizes effectively beyond its training data, robustly interprets detailed and complex text prompts, and seamlessly integrates with various generative models to yield high-quality, diverse panoramic images."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:24:25Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:24:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:24:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":19,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=%28doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21656%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21681%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21682%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21710%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21712%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21714%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21734%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21741%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21745%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21783%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21833%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21849%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21853%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21861%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21862%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22521%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2507.00057%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2507.02935%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2507.02937%22%29\u0026client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1"}}